SIGNAL(2)                      P32W Unix                       SIGNAL(2)

NAME
       signal, sigreturn - catch or ignore signals

SYNOPSIS
       #include <signal.h>

       typedef void (*sighandler_t)(int);

       sighandler_t signal(int sig, sighandler_t handler);

DESCRIPTION
       A signal is sent to a process by the kernel, when it does
       something wrong or an event it asked for happens, by its
       terminal, or by another process with kill(2). signal says
       what the calling process does with signal sig from now on:

       SIG_DFL  the default action, which for every signal is to
                terminate the process (its parent's wait(2)
                reports the signal number);
       SIG_IGN  ignore it;
       a function
                catch it: the function is called with the signal
                number as its argument, and when it returns the
                process carries on where it was interrupted.

       The signals (V7's numbers, and 4.3BSD's for SIGWINCH; all
       but that one with the default action terminate; none dumps
       core):

       1   SIGHUP    hangup (never sent by the kernel)
       2   SIGINT    interrupt: ^C on the terminal
       3   SIGQUIT   quit: ^\ on the terminal
       4   SIGILL    illegal or privileged instruction
       5   SIGTRAP   BREAK instruction; a traced process after exec
       6   SIGABRT   abort (never sent by the kernel)
       7   SIGEMT    (never sent)
       8   SIGFPE    (never sent: there is no floating point)
       9   SIGKILL   kill: cannot be caught or ignored
       10  SIGBUS    misaligned access or bus error
       11  SIGSEGV   access to an unmapped or read-only address
       12  SIGSYS    (never sent: a bad call gives ENOSYS)
       13  SIGPIPE   write on a pipe with no reader
       14  SIGALRM   alarm clock (alarm(2))
       15  SIGTERM   software termination, kill(1)'s default
       28  SIGWINCH  the terminal's size has changed (ioctl(2)'s
                     TIOCSWINSZ): sent to every process whose
                     controlling terminal it is. Not caught, it
                     does nothing: the process is not ended, and
                     no call of its is interrupted

       NSIG is 32, but the numbers from 16 to 27 and from 29 to 31
       are not used: kill(1) refuses them. (The kernel does not:
       one of them sent with kill(2) ends a process that does not
       catch it, as any signal but SIGWINCH does.)

       ^C and ^\ go to every process whose controlling terminal it
       is. When a process is killed by a fault signal (SIGILL,
       SIGTRAP, SIGBUS, SIGSEGV) that it does not catch, the kernel
       prints a line on the console with the process ID, name,
       cause, pc and faulting address.

       As in V7, a caught signal is reset to SIG_DFL just before
       the handler is called; a handler that wants the next one
       too must call signal again. A system call that was waiting
       (pause, wait, terminal or pipe I/O) when a caught signal
       came returns -1 with errno EINTR after the handler returns;
       calls are not restarted.

       After fork(2) the child has the parent's signal actions.
       exec(2) resets caught signals to SIG_DFL and keeps ignored
       ones ignored, so a program started with SIGINT ignored keeps
       ignoring it.

       How catching works. The library's signal passes the kernel
       a third argument, the address of its trampoline __sigtramp.
       To deliver a caught signal the kernel saves the interrupted
       registers (the trap frame) on the user stack and enters
       __sigtramp with r1 the signal and r2 the handler. The
       trampoline calls the handler, then makes system call 66,
       sigreturn, which reloads the saved frame. sigreturn is for
       the trampoline only; if the frame cannot be read the process
       is killed with SIGSEGV.

RETURN VALUE
       The previous action for sig; SIG_ERR (-1) on an error, with
       errno set.

ERRORS
       EINVAL  sig is not between 1 and 15, or is SIGKILL.

SEE ALSO
       kill(2), alarm(2), pause(2), ptrace(2), wait(2), kill(1),
       setjmp(3)

BUGS
       There is no sigaction, no signal masks or blocking, and no
       SIGCHLD, SIGSTOP, SIGTSTP, SIGCONT, SIGUSR1 or SIGUSR2. Only
       one signal can be pending; a second replaces the first. A
       signal arriving while its handler runs (before signal is
       called again) takes the default action and kills the
       process. Every signal, SIGHUP included, sends a process
       away with no core dump. abort (stdlib(3)) exits with status 134
       instead of sending SIGABRT.

       Ignoring a fault signal (SIGILL, SIGBUS, SIGSEGV, SIGTRAP)
       is a trap: the faulting instruction runs again and faults
       again for ever, the console filling with messages. Catch it
       or leave it at SIG_DFL.
